A||||4||GT Bastard||How much do I need to shave the heads for a gain of 1 point of compression?||||5||
Z||000000||GT Bastard||01-13-2001||07:45 PM||||My dad mention that it takes something like .60 to raise your compression 1 point on a smallblock.  A machinist said the V6 might be a different story.  By my calculations, 1 extra point of compression would put me exactly where I want to be.  I know that I can safely mill .20 over(that's what I milled on my old GT(1FST2M6's car.) How bout .40 over and shorter gasket?||||reg||5
Z||000001||Cooter||01-13-2001||08:33 PM||||I don't know the answer to your question- but if you do have the heads shaved that much, make sure you have the intake machined to fit the heads. When the heads sit lower on the block, it changes some of the angle that the stuff meets at and the intake could also bottom out against the ends of the block before it is tight on the gasket.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Formula 18||01-13-2001||09:26 PM||||Excessive milling of the head can lead to head gasket failure unless the intake manifold is also machined. For this reason, milling of the head should be kept to the minimum required to true the deck surface. Also note that machining the manifold to match the head may result in failure of the valve cover gaskets. <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||terryk||01-14-2001||01:52 AM||||According to GM .030" is the limit otherwise the water jacket gets too thin. <P>I know one person that milled .060" and the intake sits to far down on the block such that the sealing at each end is poor. The intake was milled to match.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000004||FIEROCIOUS||01-14-2001||04:45 PM||||I read, and I can't for the life of me remember were, that the 60* heads can be milled a maximum of .020 due to the intake angle I think it would be possible for more if the intake both block side and intake side were milled as well. Ask you machinist if he has the proper geometry for a 60* engine. Most machine shops are set up for 90* engines and the ratios are differant.||||reg||1
